--TEST--
`Core\Validate::isDomain` accepts a name exactly when every label of it is accepted as a name of its own and the total stays inside RFC 1123 § 2.1's 253 bytes — so the number of labels is bounded by nothing else, and the last width accepted sits beside the first refused — docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 12

--FILE--
<?nvs
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/validate.rs`'s module doc states `isDomain` as "one or
// more labels joined by single `.`" plus two byte limits, and the corpus
// already pins what a *label* is (`validate-domain-labels-are-ldh-and-agree-with-the-email-domain.nvst`,
// code point by code point) and what the limits are
// (`validate-length-limits-are-pinned-on-both-sides.nvst`, 63 and 253 on both
// sides). What neither asks is the sentence that joins them: that the member is
// the **conjunction** over the labels and nothing more, so every rule about
// dots is the label rule seen from outside, and the depth of a name is capped
// only by its length in bytes.
final class Host {
    public static function yn(bool $b): string {
        return $b ? "y" : "n";
    }

    // Each label asked as a name in its own right. A single label is a legal
    // hostname here — the module doc's `localhost` divergence from `isEmail` —
    // which is what makes this decomposition well formed at all.
    public static function everyLabelIsADomain(string $d): bool {
        bool $all = true;
        foreach (Core\Str::split($d, ".") as string $label) {
            if (!Core\Validate::isDomain($label)) {
                $all = false;
            }
        }
        return $all;
    }
}

// Fourteen names, all far inside 253 bytes, so the only rule that can decide
// any of them is the label rule. Every dot-shaped refusal in the corpus is
// here — leading, trailing, doubled — and each is *predicted* by an empty
// label failing on its own rather than asserted as a rule about dots.
array<string> $names = [
    "a.test",
    "a-b.c-d.test",
    "localhost",
    "1.2.3.4",
    "x.y.z.w.example.test",
    "A.TEST",
    "-a.test",
    "a.-b.test",
    "a.b-.test",
    "a..test",
    "a.test.",
    ".a.test",
    "a_b.test",
    "ex\u{e4}mple.test",
];

int $agree = 0;
string $answers = "";
foreach ($names as string $d) {
    bool $whole = Core\Validate::isDomain($d);
    if ($whole == Host::everyLabelIsADomain($d)) {
        $agree = $agree + 1;
    }
    $answers = $answers . Host::yn($whole);
}
echo "answers ", $answers, "\n";
echo "agree ", $agree as string, "\n";

// Depth is free until the total is not. A name of `n` single-byte labels is
// `2n - 1` bytes, so it is legal for as long as that stays inside 253 — which
// is 127 labels, and the sweep counts them rather than the case asserting a
// label ceiling that does not exist. A member that capped the depth at any of
// the numbers a DNS implementation might reach for answers below 127 here.
int $deep = 0;
string $chain = "a";
int $n = 1;
for (; $n <= 140; $n = $n + 1) {
    if (Core\Validate::isDomain($chain)) {
        $deep = $deep + 1;
    }
    $chain = $chain . ".a";
}
echo "depth ", $deep as string, "\n";

// The same bound named on both sides, and named as a byte count rather than a
// label count: 127 labels is 253 bytes and is a name, 128 labels is 255 bytes
// and is not — and one label of 63 bytes with 95 single-byte labels after it
// is 253 bytes as well, so it is accepted for the identical reason with a
// third of the depth.
string $l127 = "a";
int $i = 1;
for (; $i < 127; $i = $i + 1) {
    $l127 = $l127 . ".a";
}
string $l128 = $l127 . ".a";

string $wide = Core\Str::repeat("b", 63 as uint);
int $k = 0;
for (; $k < 95; $k = $k + 1) {
    $wide = $wide . ".a";
}

echo "total ",
     Host::yn(Core\Validate::isDomain($l127)),
     Host::yn(Core\Validate::isDomain($l128)),
     Host::yn(Core\Validate::isDomain($wide)),
     Host::yn(Core\Validate::isDomain($wide . ".a")),
     "\n";

echo "bytes ",
     Core\Bytes::length($l127 as bytes) as string, "|",
     Core\Bytes::length($l128 as bytes) as string, "|",
     Core\Bytes::length($wide as bytes) as string,
     "\n";
--EXPECT--
answers yyyyyynnnnnnnn
agree 14
depth 127
total ynyn
bytes 253|255|253
